// Debug build settings for the MyApp target, wired in by project.yml's configFiles.
//
// A build for your own iPhone (`just run-device`) needs a development team. It lives
// only in Config/Local.xcconfig, which you create yourself, holding one line:
//
//   DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = <your team ID>
//
// The team ID is the one Xcode › Settings › Accounts shows for your Apple account.
// Local.xcconfig is gitignored and the commit-time guard refuses it: a team ID belongs
// to one person's account, and this repository may be public. Simulator builds sign
// ad hoc and need no team, so the file is optional (hence `#include?`).
//
// Only Debug includes it: `just run-device` builds Debug, and the unsigned Release
// device build (`just build-device`, CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO) takes no team, so no
// signed Release route to a device exists. An app that adds distribution revisits this.
// See docs/running-on-device.md.

#include? "Local.xcconfig"

// The API every networked feature calls, read by Core's AppConfiguration through the
// APIBaseURL key project.yml's info.properties expands. Debug points at staging; the
// `.invalid` host is a placeholder that never resolves (RFC 6761) until the app names
// its own server. `//` starts an xcconfig comment, so the URL's `//` is written
// `/$()/`: an empty expansion between the slashes. Never a secret here — this file is
// committed and the value ships in Info.plist (AppConfiguration's doc says where one goes).
API_BASE_URL = https:/$()/api.staging.invalid/v1
